/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Treat each independent source as an input x and any chosen voltage or current as the output y.
   - Resistors (v = Ri), inductors (v = L di/dt) and capacitors (i = C dv/dt) are linear elements: each relation is linear in the element's own variables.
   - y = a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ + … + aNxN
   - y is any voltage or current in the circuit; xk is the value of independent source k; ak is a constant set by the resistances and dependent-source gains; N is the number of independent sources.
   - Why it holds: nodal or mesh analysis of a linear circuit gives equations whose coefficients are fixed numbers and whose right sides contain only the source values.
   - y′ is the response with only the first independent source on, y″ with only the second, and so on.
   - Sources may also be switched on in groups, as long as every source is on in exactly one run.
   - Power does not superpose.
   - Dependent sources must not be switched off.
   - Nonlinear elements break it.
*/
