/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 12 V source in series with 6 Ω feeds terminal a; iₓ is the current in the 6 Ω towards a. A 12 Ω resistor joins a to b (the reference), and a current-controlled current source of 0.5iₓ drives current from b into a. Find the Thevenin equivalent at a-b and check Rth with a test source.
   1. Open circuit, v = voltage of a: KCL at a gives iₓ + 0.5iₓ = v/12 with iₓ = (12 − v)/6. So 1.5(12 − v)/6 = v/12; times 12: 3(12 − v) = v, so v = 9 V. Vth = 9 V.
   2. Short circuit (a joined to b): v = 0, so iₓ = 12/6 = 2 A, the 12 Ω carries nothing, and Isc = iₓ + 0.5iₓ = 3 A.
   3. Rth = Voc/Isc = 9/3 = 3 Ω.
   4. Test-source check: short the 12 V source, keep the controlled source, apply Vt at a. Now iₓ = (0 − Vt)/6 = −Vt/6. KCL at a: It + iₓ + 0.5iₓ = Vt/12, so It = Vt/12 + 1.5Vt/6 = Vt/12 + 3Vt/12 = Vt/3. Rth = 3 Ω, as before.
   5. Wrong method for comparison: switching off the controlled source gives 6 ∥ 12 = 4 Ω.
   6. A 6 Ω load would draw 9/(3 + 6) = 1 A.
   Answer: Vth = 9 V, Isc = 3 A, Rth = 3 Ω (not 4 Ω); a 6 Ω load draws 1 A. */
