/* book exam traps: Switching sources off the wrong way. A voltage source becomes a short and a current source an open; reversing them changes Rth. | Calling a resistor of the network the load. Only the element you remove is the load; in worked example 1 the 12 Ω and 1 Ω stay inside. | Switching off a dependent source when finding Rth. In worked example 2 that gives 4 Ω instead of 3 Ω. | Getting the sign of IN wrong. Take it as the current leaving a through the short, the same direction in which Vth (a positive) drives a load. | Using the equivalent to find power lost inside the original network. The equivalent is exact only at the terminals; its Rth loss is not the network's internal loss. */
